          "title": "The Catholic Church and Divine Retribution",
          "content": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[1.1]</span> Throughout the medieval era (c1250–c1500), the Roman Catholic Church was the supreme ideological authority across Western Europe. Society was profoundly religious, and people firmly believed that Almighty God controlled every aspect of daily life, health, and sickness.<br><br><span class=\"para-ref\">[1.2]</span> Illness was widely explained as divine retribution: God sending disease to punish individuals for personal sins (such as greed, lust, or blasphemy), or testing a believer's spiritual faith like the biblical Job. The Church taught that the physical body was merely a temporary vessel for the immortal soul, meaning spiritual salvation was infinitely more important than bodily cure.",
