          "theme": "Leprosy: The Visible Mark of Spiritual Sickness",
          "title": "Leprosy: The Visible Mark of Spiritual Sickness",
          "content": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[3.1]</span> Few diseases illustrated the fusion of physical illness and religious moral judgment as vividly as **leprosy**. Caused by a painful bacterial infection that destroyed nerves and facial skin, leprosy was viewed by contemporaries as God's visible punishment for severe moral corruption.<br><br><span class=\"para-ref\">[3.2]</span> Sufferers were officially declared legally dead by Church courts, forced to wear hooded gray cloaks, and commanded to ring hand bells to warn healthy people away. Banished from towns, they were housed in specialized isolated monastic lazars (**leper houses**) situated outside city walls, receiving spiritual prayers rather than clinical cures.",
