          "theme": "Astrology, Planetary Conjunctions, and Bad Air",
          "title": "Astrology, Planetary Conjunctions, and Bad Air",
          "content": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[4.1]</span> Alongside direct divine action, medieval society relied heavily upon **astrology** to explain the timing of unexpected diseases. The position and movements of stars and planets were believed to exert powerful supernatural influences over human bodies.<br><br><span class=\"para-ref\">[4.2]</span> University-trained physicians routinely carried *vade mecum* pocketbooks containing astrological calendars and **Zodiac Man** charts (<span class=\"archival-meta-tag\">Source A</span>), which mapped each sign of the zodiac to specific anatomical organs. Conjunctions of planets (especially Saturn and Mars) were blamed for generating corrupt, poisonous air (**miasma**), warning physicians against operating on body parts ruled by unfavourable astrological signs.",
