          "act_title": "Stage 5: Censorship & The Ban on Dissection — Why did church enforcement of Galen prevent medical progress?",
          "theme": "The Catholic Church's Monopoly on Medical Knowledge",
          "title": "The Catholic Church's Monopoly on Medical Knowledge",
          "content": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[5.1]</span> The Catholic Church maintained a strict monopoly over medieval intellectual life. Monks in monastic scriptoria copied medical manuscripts entirely by hand in Latin, controlling which texts were preserved and taught in universities.<br><br><span class=\"para-ref\">[5.2]</span> The Church promoted ancient Greek and Roman medical writers, particularly **Galen**, because his philosophical texts argued the human body was designed with divine purpose by a single Creator. Because Galen’s ideas harmonized with Christian theology, criticizing Galen was condemned as heresy. Independent clinical dissection and scientific experimentation were strictly forbidden, stifling new medical discovery for centuries.",
