          "act_title": "Stage 1: Hippocrates & Natural Observation — How did Hippocrates separate medicine from superstition?",
          "theme": "Hippocrates and the Theory of the Four Humours",
          "title": "Hippocrates and the Theory of the Four Humours",
          "content": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[1.1]</span> Alongside supernatural beliefs, medieval physicians relied upon rational, natural theories formulated by ancient Greek physician **Hippocrates** (c460–c370 BC). Hippocrates rejected demonic explanations, arguing health depended upon the balance of four bodily fluids (**humours**).<br><br><span class=\"para-ref\">[1.2]</span> These humours were **Blood** (hot and wet, linked to spring), **Phlegm** (cold and wet, linked to winter), **Yellow Bile** (hot and dry, linked to summer), and **Black Bile** (cold and dry, linked to autumn). Hippocrates taught that when humours were balanced, a person enjoyed perfect health; illness occurred when one humour became excessive, deficient, or corrupt.",
          "text": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[1.1]</span> Alongside supernatural beliefs, medieval physicians relied upon rational, natural theories formulated by ancient Greek physician **Hippocrates** (c460–c370 BC). Hippocrates rejected demonic explanations, arguing health depended upon the balance of four bodily fluids (**humours**).<br><br><span class=\"para-ref\">[1.2]</span> These humours were **Blood** (hot and wet, linked to spring), **Phlegm** (cold and wet, linked to winter), **Yellow Bile** (hot and dry, linked to summer), and **Black Bile** (cold and dry, linked to autumn). Hippocrates taught that when humours were balanced, a person enjoyed perfect health; illness occurred when one humour became excessive, deficient, or corrupt.",
