          "theme": "Hippocratic Clinical Observation and the Prognosis",
          "title": "Hippocratic Clinical Observation and the Prognosis",
          "content": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[3.1]</span> Hippocrates' greatest legacy was establishing the foundational method of **clinical observation**. Rather than consulting astrologers, Hippocrates instructed physicians to examine patients directly: recording pulse rates, skin temperature, and breathing patterns.<br><br><span class=\"para-ref\">[3.2]</span> By observing hundreds of clinical cases, physicians identified the \"crisis point\" of an illness—the turning point where a patient either overcame fever or succumbed. Furthermore, Hippocratic doctors swore the **Hippocratic Oath**, pledging ethical conduct, patient confidentiality, and non-harm, creating the moral code of professional clinical medicine.",
