          "theme": "Galen, Teleology, and the Church's Monopoly",
          "title": "Galen, Teleology, and the Church's Monopoly",
          "content": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[4.1]</span> Galen’s enduring medieval dominance was guaranteed by the medieval **Catholic Church**. Although Galen lived in pagan Rome, his philosophical writings were **teleological**: he argued that the human body was purposefully crafted in every detail by a single divine Creator, and that human dissection revealed the perfection of God's design.<br><br><span class=\"para-ref\">[4.2]</span> Because this aligned seamlessly with Christian theology, the Church adopted Galen’s texts as absolute medical scripture. The Church held a monopoly over education: monk scribes controlled manuscript copying, and universities taught medicine entirely from Latin translations of Galen. Challenging Galen was condemned as challenging God: when English scholar Roger Bacon advocated independent empirical experimentation in 1277, Church authorities imprisoned him for heresy.",
