          "theme": "Galenic Anatomy: The Consequences of Dissecting Animals",
          "title": "Galenic Anatomy: The Consequences of Dissecting Animals",
          "content": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[5.1]</span> Galen authored hundreds of medical treatises detailing human anatomy. However, because human dissection was legally and religiously forbidden in pagan Rome, Galen based his anatomical descriptions upon dissecting **pigs, apes, and dogs**.<br><br><span class=\"para-ref\">[5.2]</span> Consequently, Galen made major factual errors: claiming the human jaw consisted of two bones, the liver had five lobes, and blood was manufactured in the liver and burned as fuel by tissues. Because medieval universities forbade questioning Galen, medical students merely watched barber-surgeons dissect executed criminals to confirm Galen's written words, leaving his anatomical errors uncorrected for over a thousand years.",
