      "hook_text": "Alongside deep religious faith, medieval physicians inherited a powerful rational framework from the ancient Mediterranean: the natural medicine of Hippocrates and Galen. At its core was the Theory of the Four Humours—blood, phlegm, yellow bile, and black bile—fluids whose delicate balance determined not only physical health, but human personality and temperament. Galen expanded this into the Theory of Opposites, giving university-trained doctors a systematic logic for prescribing diets and remedies to counteract bodily excess. Armed with glass matula flasks to scrutinize urine charts and vigilant against the noxious stench of poisonous miasmas, medieval practitioners believed they possessed a complete, unshakeable science of the human body. Why was this logical, elegant system so fiercely defended for centuries despite failing to cure epidemic disease?",
      "learning_objectives": [
        "Explain how Hippocrates and Galen established natural, rational explanations for disease through clinical observation.",
        "Analyze the Theory of the Four Humours and Galen’s Theory of Opposites as systems for diagnosing and treating illness.",
        "Assess the diagnostic use of uroscopy (matula flask) and evaluate the role of miasma theory in medieval epidemiology."
      ],
