      "vocab_cloze_text": "Rational medieval medicine was anchored in the ancient Greek concept of the [Four Humours], which maintained that good health required an exact balance between blood, phlegm, yellow bile, and black bile. Building upon this, Claudius Galen introduced the [Theory of Opposites], arguing that cold and wet sicknesses (like winter colds) should be cured with hot and dry foods like pepper. Trainee physicians swore the sacred [Hippocratic Oath], pledging ethical conduct and confidentiality, and learned the practice of [Clinical Observation] to monitor fever patterns and examine urine flasks at the patient's bedside. However, medical training soon ossified into rigid [Galenic Dogma], because the Roman Catholic Church heavily promoted Galen's anatomical texts as divine proof that God had engineered the human body for a purposeful creator.",
