          "title": "Phlebotomy: Restoring Balance through Bloodletting",
          "content": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[2.1]</span> The most common clinical treatment in medieval Britain was **phlebotomy** (bloodletting). Physicians believed fevers and inflammations were caused by an excess of blood (*plethora*), requiring controlled evacuation to restore humoral harmony.<br><br><span class=\"para-ref\">[2.2]</span> Bloodletting was performed in three ways: **venesection** (cutting a vein with a fleam or lancet); **cupping** (heating glass cups over scratched skin to draw blood); and **leeches** collected from marshes. Physicians consulted **Vein Man** charts to select the precise vein corresponding to the diseased organ, warning against phlebotomy when astrological alignments were unfavorable.",
