          "theme": "Medieval Prevention and the Regimen Sanitatis",
          "title": "Medieval Prevention and the Regimen Sanitatis",
          "content": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[5.1]</span> Medieval medicine placed immense emphasis upon prevention, governed by the **Regimen Sanitatis** (rule of health)—a set of dietary and lifestyle guidelines written by Salernitan physicians.<br><br><span class=\"para-ref\">[5.2]</span> The Regimen urged individuals to balance the \"Six Non-Naturals\": moderate food, adequate sleep, fresh air, exercise, emotional calmness, and regular bathing. To ward off airborne **miasma**, citizens carried fragrant herbs, clove-studded oranges (**pomanders**), and burned pine resin indoors, believing pleasant aromas neutralized disease-carrying foul air.",
