      "hook_text": "To survive illness in medieval England, a patient entered a world where spiritual penance and violent physical intervention went hand in hand. If an affliction was sent by God, the first line of defense was prayer, fasting, pilgrimages to holy shrines, or self-flagellation to earn divine mercy. Yet physicians and barber-surgeons also intervened directly in the flesh: draining veins through phlebotomy, applying blood-sucking leeches, and administering violent herbal emetics or pewter clysters to purge corrupt humours from the digestive tract. The wealthy protected themselves with bespoke lifestyle regimens (the Regimen Sanitatis) and complex apothecary electuaries like Theriac, boasting dozens of exotic ingredients. Why did ordinary people eagerly submit to excruciating treatments that frequently weakened the body rather than healing it?",
