      "vocab_cloze_text": "To restore internal balance when humours became corrupted, medieval healers relied primarily on [Phlebotomy], drawing excess blood through vein incision, cupping, or applying leeches. Patients also underwent vigorous [Purging] with violent emetics, bitter laxatives, and enema clysters to expel offending bile from their digestive tracts. For daily preventative hygiene, physicians compiled the [Regimen Sanitatis], a comprehensive code of conduct advising wealthy citizens on wholesome diet, restful sleep, emotional temperance, and seasonal exercise. When deadly pestilence struck, apothecaries compounded [Theriac], a complex antidotal paste containing up to seventy ingredients including snake flesh and opium. Most common illnesses were treated using home remedies drawn from the folk [Materia Medica], which catalogued curative herbs, roots, minerals, and animal compounds.",
