          "act_title": "Stage 1: University-Trained Physicians — Why were university physicians accessible only to the ultra-wealthy?",
          "theme": "University-Trained Physicians: Theory Over Practice",
          "title": "University-Trained Physicians: Theory Over Practice",
          "content": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[1.1]</span> Medieval healthcare was strictly stratified by wealth and class. At the pinnacle were university-trained **physicians**. Educated for up to ten years at Oxford, Cambridge, Paris, or Montpellier, physicians were scholastic academics who rarely touched patients directly.<br><br><span class=\"para-ref\">[1.2]</span> They diagnosed ailments from ancient Greek and Arab texts in Latin, inspecting urine flasks (*uroscopy*), checking pulse rates, and consulting astrological charts. Because training was lengthy and rare, physicians charged exorbitant consultation fees, making their services accessible only to royalty, aristocracy, and wealthy merchants.",
