          "act": 2,
          "act_title": "Stage 2: Guild Apothecaries & Spicers — How did apothecaries provide accessible medicines to townspeople?",
          "theme": "Guild Apothecaries and Spicers: Retail Medicine",
          "title": "Guild Apothecaries and Spicers: Retail Medicine",
          "content": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[2.1]</span> For urban merchants and tradesmen who could not afford a physician, the local **apothecary shop** was the first port of call. Apothecaries were organized into trade guilds, training through seven-year practical apprenticeships.<br><br><span class=\"para-ref\">[2.2]</span> They stocked hundreds of dried roots, herbs, minerals, and imported spices from Mediterranean trade networks. Unlike physicians who charged high academic fees, apothecaries diagnosed customers directly across the counter, mixing custom ointments, syrups, and electuaries. Though physicians viewed them with professional snobbery as mere tradesmen, ordinary citizens valued their practical, accessible pharmacy.",
