          "theme": "Barber-Surgeons and Craft Medicine",
          "title": "Barber-Surgeons and Craft Medicine",
          "content": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[3.1]</span> In medieval towns, practical surgical care was delivered by **barber-surgeons**. Possessing no university Latin education, barber-surgeons were craftsmen belonging to urban trade guilds, learning through apprenticeships with razors, lancets, and saws.<br><br><span class=\"para-ref\">[3.2]</span> They cut hair, pulled rotten teeth, dressed open wounds, set broken bones, and performed phlebotomy (bloodletting). While prestigious surgeons like John of Arderne treated wealthy knights on battlefields, ordinary barber-surgeons provided affordable, manual emergency treatments for working-class citizens who could never consult an academic physician.",
