          "act": 4,
          "act_title": "Stage 4: Monastic Hospitals: Care Not Cure — Why did monastic hospitals deliberately avoid medical treatment?",
          "theme": "Monastic Hospitals: ‘Care Not Cure’",
          "title": "Monastic Hospitals: ‘Care Not Cure’",
          "content": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[4.1]</span> By 1500, England possessed over 700 hospitals, virtually all founded, funded, and operated by monastic religious orders, including **St Bartholomew’s** (founded 1123) and **St Thomas’s** (1215) in London.<br><br><span class=\"para-ref\">[4.2]</span> These institutions were not medical treatment facilities in the modern sense. Run by monks and nuns, their guiding ethos was **\"care not cure\"**: providing shelter, warm fires, clean bedding, nourishment, and daily religious prayer to comfort souls. Crucially, medieval hospitals rejected infectious patients, pregnant women, and terminal cases to preserve cleanliness and avoid spiritual pollution.",
          "text": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[4.1]</span> By 1500, England possessed over 700 hospitals, virtually all founded, funded, and operated by monastic religious orders, including **St Bartholomew’s** (founded 1123) and **St Thomas’s** (1215) in London.<br><br><span class=\"para-ref\">[4.2]</span> These institutions were not medical treatment facilities in the modern sense. Run by monks and nuns, their guiding ethos was **\"care not cure\"**: providing shelter, warm fires, clean bedding, nourishment, and daily religious prayer to comfort souls. Crucially, medieval hospitals rejected infectious patients, pregnant women, and terminal cases to preserve cleanliness and avoid spiritual pollution.",
          "image": "/images/hotel_dieu_hospital.jpg",
