          "theme": "Domestic Healers and Wise Women",
          "title": "Domestic Healers and Wise Women",
          "content": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[5.1]</span> For the vast majority of the population living in rural peasant villages, healthcare began and ended in the home. Peasant families relied upon **female domestic healers**—mothers, grandmothers, and local \"wise women\".<br><br><span class=\"para-ref\">[5.2]</span> Drawing upon traditional folklore passed down through generations, women cultivated herb gardens, brewing remedies from willow bark (pain relief), mint (digestion), and chamomile. Village wise women also acted as midwives delivering babies, bone-setters, and herbal pharmacists, providing free or barter-based care that formed the true foundation of medieval community medicine.",
