      "vocab_cloze_text": "Medical care in medieval society was divided among very different practitioners. Monks and nuns ran the [Monastic Infirmary], providing quiet shelter, clean bedding, herbal broths, and spiritual solace to elderly or chronically ill pilgrims rather than curative medical surgery. Manual treatments fell to the [Barber-Surgeon], who used razor-sharp blades to amputate gangrenous limbs, extract decayed teeth, and perform phlebotomy for modest fees. Botanical cures and ointments were compounded and sold by the [Apothecary], who trained in guilds to mix herbs, wines, and minerals into commercial tinctures. The elite social tier was occupied by the [University-Trained Physician], who charged high fees to rich clients for academic consultations based on astrological charts and pulse readings without ever performing physical surgery. In remote rural hamlets, most peasant families turned to the local [Wise Woman], whose generationally inherited herbal remedies, bone-setting, and midwifery provided vital frontline care.",
