          "title": "Arrival of the Great Pestilence (June 1348)",
          "content": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[1.1]</span> In June 1348, the **Black Death** (*Yersinia pestis*) arrived in England aboard trading ships docking at Melcombe Regis (Weymouth), Dorset. Originating in Central Asia, the plague swept relentlessly along trade routes across Europe, striking London by autumn 1348.<br><br><span class=\"para-ref\">[1.2]</span> Contemporaries confronted an apocalyptic disaster beyond comprehension. Within eighteen months, the epidemic annihilated between **30% and 45% of England’s population**, leaving entire villages deserted and corpses piled unburied in fields, sparking economic and social turmoil.",
