          "content": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[2.1]</span> The Black Death struck in two terrifying forms. The commonest was **bubonic plague**, transmitted by fleas living on black rats (*Rattus rattus*). Victims developed high fever, violent vomiting, and agonizing, swollen lymph glands called **buboes** in the groin and armpits.<br><br><span class=\"para-ref\">[2.2]</span> Dark purple and black spots appeared on the skin as internal bleeding set in. The deadlier variation was **pneumonic plague**, spread directly through airborne cough droplets into lungs. Victims coughed up blood and suffocated within twenty-four hours, carrying a terrifying 100% mortality rate.",
