          "theme": "Desperate Treatments and Hopeless Cures",
          "title": "Desperate Treatments and Hopeless Cures",
          "content": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[4.1]</span> Medieval physicians and domestic healers were completely powerless against the plague. Treatments were desperate and ineffective: barber-surgeons bled patients until they fainted, which weakened immune systems and accelerated death.<br><br><span class=\"para-ref\">[4.2]</span> Healers lanced and drained buboes with hot irons, strapped living plumed chickens to swellings to \"draw out poison,\" and administered costly potions of crushed emeralds, arsenic, and Venice Treacle. Wealthy families fled infected cities into the countryside, while poor citizens burned aromatic pine resin indoors to purify air.",
