      "hook_text": "In June 1348, a merchant cog from Gascony docked at the Dorset port of Melcombe Regis, carrying a cargo far deadlier than wine: the Black Death. Within months, the epidemic swept across England with terrifying speed, extinguishing between a third and a half of the entire population. Victims suffered excruciating egg-sized buboes in the armpits and groin before spitting black blood in lethal pneumonic agonies. Faced with an apocalypse, contemporary medicine collapsed into panic: doctors lanced swellings with red-hot irons, strapped live chickens to open sores, or prescribed drinking crushed emeralds. Civic authorities banned street muck and King Edward III ordered London’s aldermen to clean the gutters to disperse corrupt miasma, but the dying continued unabated. How did the catastrophic demographic collapse of 1348 permanently shatter the feudal foundations of medieval society?",
