      "vocab_cloze_text": "In the scorching summer of 1348, the lethal pandemic known as the [Black Death] reached the southern ports of Dorset and swept across England, killing roughly half the population. Terrified victims suffered fever, violent vomiting, and hideous egg-sized [Buboes] swelling painfully in the armpits and groin that oozed pus before turning black. To check the terrifying airborne contagion, wealthy gentlemen carried a sweet-smelling [Pomander] filled with dried lavender and rose petals to ward off the deadly miasma. Desperate town magistrates enforced strict [Quarantine], boarding up infected households and burning pitch barrels in city alleys to purify polluted air. In London, municipal authorities issued the [Winchester Cemetery Order], consecrating vast communal burial grounds outside the city gates at West Smithfield and Spitalfields to bury the overwhelming mountain of corpses in deep lime trenches.",
