          "theme": "The Printing Press and the End of Monastic Monopoly",
          "content": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[1.1]</span> Around 1440, Johannes Gutenberg developed movable metal type, sparking an information revolution across Europe. Introduced to England by William Caxton in 1476, the printing press permanently shattered the Catholic Church's monopoly over written manuscripts.<br><br><span class=\"para-ref\">[1.2]</span> Medical texts no longer took months to copy by hand in monastic scriptoria; thousands of identical copies were produced rapidly and cheaply. Crucially, anatomical diagrams, surgical engravings, and clinical observations were replicated with exact visual precision, allowing revolutionary scientific discoveries to circulate across Europe without scribal distortion or Church censorship.",
