          "theme": "Renaissance Humanism: Returning to Original Greek Sources",
          "content": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[2.1]</span> Beginning in fifteenth-century Italy, Renaissance Humanism championed human intellectual potential, direct observation, and a return to original classical manuscripts. Humanist scholars bypassed medieval Latin translations, studying original Greek and Hebrew texts.<br><br><span class=\"para-ref\">[2.2]</span> Scholars discovered that centuries of Arab and Christian monk copying had introduced major translation errors into Galen’s works. More importantly, humanism fostered a \"spirit of inquiry\": encouraging natural philosophers to verify ancient claims by direct physical observation rather than blindly memorizing medical orthodoxy.",
