          "theme": "The Royal Society and the Motto ‘Nullius in Verba’",
          "content": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[3.1]</span> In November 1660, natural philosophers—including Christopher Wren, Robert Boyle, and John Wilkins—founded the **Royal Society** in London, receiving a royal charter from King Charles II in 1662.<br><br><span class=\"para-ref\">[3.2]</span> The Royal Society adopted the famous Latin motto **'Nullius in Verba'** (*'Take nobody's word for it'*). This bold declaration demanded that scientific truth must be established through repeatable physical experiments, empirical proof, and mathematical observation, rejecting unquestioned reliance on ancient authorities like Galen and Aristotle.",
