          "text": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[2.1]</span> In 1676, Sydenham published his masterpiece *Observationes Medicae*, introducing a revolutionary concept: diseases were distinct biological entities, just as botanists classified plants into species.<br><br><span class=\"para-ref\">[2.2]</span> For 1,500 years, Galenic doctrine held that each patient had a unique humoral balance. Sydenham proved that diseases had specific, predictable patterns regardless of the patient's individual personality. He distinguished scarlet fever from measles and accurately described chronic conditions like gout, establishing modern clinical diagnosis.",
