          "text": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[4.1]</span> Publication of *Fabrica* unleashed ferocious academic hostility. Conservative professors, horrified that an upstart youth had declared Galen wrong, launched vicious attacks.<br><br><span class=\"para-ref\">[4.2]</span> Vesalius's former Paris teacher, Jacobus Sylvius, denounced him as a \"madman whose filth defiles European medicine,\" claiming the human body must have changed since Roman times rather than admitting Galen erred. Frustrated by academic stubbornness, Vesalius burned his manuscripts and became court physician to Emperor Charles V.",
