          "title": "Source B (Written Extract): Andreas Vesalius, Preface to De Humani Corporis Fabrica (1543)",
          "text": "Those who teach anatomy today sit aloft in a high chair like rooks, croaking with lofty pride about things they have never seen, but only learned by rote from the books of others. Meanwhile, an ignorant barber-surgeon hacketh at the cadaver without understanding what he uncovereth. Thus all things are taught wrongly in the schools, and days are wasted in ridiculous questions. I was compelled to lay my own hand to the work, dissecting human bodies myself, whereby I discovered more than two hundred errors in the anatomical writings of the divine Galen.",
