          "content": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[5.1]</span> Although Harvey founded modern physiology, his immediate impact on bedside therapeutics was virtually non-existent: knowing blood circulated did not cure fevers or heal wounds.<br><br><span class=\"para-ref\">[5.2]</span> Doctors continued bloodletting with leeches for another two centuries, and early attempts at blood transfusions triggered fatal clotting reactions. Harvey's true significance was scientific: proving that the human body operated like a mechanical system governed by physical laws that could be quantified mathematically.",
