          "theme": "Continuity of Ideas: Sin, Miasma, and Astrology",
          "content": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[2.1]</span> Despite the anatomical breakthroughs of Vesalius and Harvey, seventeenth-century explanations for the plague were almost identical to 1348.<br><br><span class=\"para-ref\">[2.2]</span> Most citizens viewed the epidemic as God's divine wrath punishing London's moral wickedness. Others blamed poisonous **miasma** rising from filthy cesspools, or pointed to unusual planetary alignments. Lacking any concept of bacteria or rat fleas, medical understanding showed profound continuity with the Middle Ages.",
          "image": "/images/london_bill_of_mortality_1665.jpg",
