      "hook_text": "In 1860, the greatest scientific minds in Europe gathered at the Sorbonne in Paris to witness a duel of ideas. For centuries, orthodox science taught that rotting meat magically generated maggots and foul swamp water spontaneously spawned microbes out of nothing. Armed with glass flasks drawn out into elegant, curving swan necks, a French industrial chemist named Louis Pasteur set out to prove that life only comes from life. Yet, when Pasteur announced that invisible, airborne microorganisms invaded human bodies to cause disease, the British medical establishment sneered. It took twenty years of forensic laboratory detective work by a German country doctor named Robert Koch—staining bacteria with purple industrial dyes and photographing them under microscope lenses—to finally smash two thousand years of superstition and bad smells. How did Pasteur's Germ Theory and Koch's bacteriological discoveries finally dismantle the ancient theory of miasma?",
