          "text": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[1.1]</span> In the mid-nineteenth century, medical science remained trapped in confusion regarding disease causation. Although Antonie van Leeuwenhoek had observed microscopic organisms in 1676, doctors assumed they were harmless by-products of decay. The dominant orthodoxy was **Spontaneous Generation**—the belief that rotting matter naturally spawned living microbes out of decomposition. Alongside this, the medical profession clung tenaciously to **miasma theory**, arguing that poisonous vapors rising from filth and open cesspools corrupted the human bloodstream.<br><br><span class=\"para-ref\">[1.2]</span> In 1859, the French Academy of Sciences offered a prize to anyone who could solve spontaneous generation through experimentation. French chemist **Louis Pasteur**, hired by industrial brewers to investigate why beer and wine soured, proved that fermentation was caused by living yeasts, while souring was caused by unwanted microbes. Pasteur hypothesized that microbes were living organisms carried invisibly through the atmosphere.",
