          "title": "Louis Pasteur & The Germ Theory Breakthrough (1861)",
          "theme_heading": "Louis Pasteur & The Germ Theory Breakthrough (1861)",
          "text": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[2.1]</span> In 1861, Pasteur conducted his legendary **swan-neck flask experiments**. He boiled nutrient broth inside glass flasks to destroy pre-existing microbial life, drawing the flask necks into curving S-shapes (<span class=\"archival-meta-tag\">Source A</span>). Air circulated freely through the open end, but airborne dust and bacteria settled in the downward curve of the neck. The broth inside remained fresh and sterile indefinitely.<br><br><span class=\"para-ref\">[2.2]</span> However, when the neck was snapped off or the flask was tilted so liquid washed into the curve, bacteria multiplied rapidly, turning the broth cloudy and rotten within hours. In 1861, Pasteur published his landmark **Germ Theory**, establishing that life only arises from pre-existing life and that airborne microbes cause fermentation and decay. Pasteur made the revolutionary deduction: if microscopic germs could turn wine sour, they could also invade human bodies and cause contagious infectious disease.",
