          "content": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[3.1]</span> Despite Pasteur’s breakthrough, British doctors initially rejected Germ Theory. Pasteur was an industrial chemist, not a qualified physician; while he proved germs soured beer, he had not identified which specific germ caused any human illness. Prominent British authorities, led by Dr Henry Charlton Bastian, fiercely opposed Germ Theory, arguing that because bacteria lived harmlessly inside healthy throats and guts, it was absurd to blame them for lethal epidemics.<br><br><span class=\"para-ref\">[3.2]</span> British skepticism finally broke down through the work of Irish physicist **John Tyndall**. Conducting precision light-beam experiments in sealed optical chambers between 1869 and 1876, Tyndall proved that optically pure, dust-free air never produced bacterial growth, while ordinary atmospheric dust teemed with living bacterial spores. Tyndall’s lectures to the Royal Society provided undeniable physical proof that airborne microbes—not spontaneous generation—caused contamination.",
