          "title": "Robert Koch & Specific Bacteria (1876–1882)",
          "theme_heading": "Robert Koch & Specific Bacteria (1876–1882)",
          "text": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[4.1]</span> The missing medical proof was delivered by German country doctor **Robert Koch**, who transformed bacteriology into an exact science through four technical breakthroughs: solid culture media (agar jelly and potato slices) to grow pure colonies; methyl violet synthetic dyes to stain transparent bacteria; oil-immersion lenses; and photography (photomicrography) to document findings precisely.<br><br><span class=\"para-ref\">[4.2]</span> In 1876, Koch identified the specific bacterium causing **anthrax**, proving unequivocally that distinct germs cause specific diseases. In 1882, he discovered the microbe causing **tuberculosis**, a dreaded disease that killed one in seven people, followed by **cholera** in 1883 (<span class=\"archival-meta-tag\">Source C</span>). Koch formulated \"Koch’s Postulates\"—a strict forensic standard proving a specific microbe causes a particular disease—inspiring an international generation of \"microbe hunters\" across Europe.",
          "image": "/images/robert_koch_laboratory.jpg",
          "image_alt": "Robert Koch's scientific plate 439 illustrating stained bacterial specimens and photographic methods",
          "caption": "Dr Robert Koch in his Berlin Laboratory (c. 1885): Koch transformed microbiology through four innovations: isolating pure bacterial colonies in flat glass Petri dishes using solid nutrient agar, staining transparent microbes with synthetic industrial aniline dyes, and examining specimen slides with precision oil-immersion Zeiss microscopes.",
