      "vocab_cloze_text": "For centuries, physicians believed in [Spontaneous Generation], assuming that decaying organic matter created microbes out of nothing. In 1861, French chemist Louis Pasteur published his groundbreaking [Germ Theory (1861)], using swan-neck flasks to prove that airborne microbes caused decay. However, British doctors resisted because Pasteur was a chemist. The deadlock was broken by German physician Robert Koch, who grew pure bacterial cultures on [Agar Jelly] and stained microbes with synthetic [Aniline Dyes]. In 1882, Koch published [Koch’s Postulates (1882)], establishing four scientific rules to prove that specific microbes caused specific diseases.",
