          "content": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[2.1]</span> The breakthrough came from **Edward Jenner**, an observant country doctor working in Berkeley, Gloucestershire. Jenner investigated local rural folklore that dairymaids who contracted **cowpox**—a mild blistering infection caught from cows’ udders—never suffered from deadly smallpox. Jenner concluded that cowpox granted cross-immunity against smallpox.<br><br><span class=\"para-ref\">[2.2]</span> In May 1796, Jenner conducted his historic experiment: extracting pus from a cowpox lesion on milkmaid Sarah Nelmes, he inoculated eight-year-old **James Phipps** (<span class=\"archival-meta-tag\">Source A</span>). After Phipps recovered from mild cowpox, Jenner inoculated him with live smallpox fluid six weeks later. Phipps developed zero smallpox symptoms. Jenner repeated the experiment on multiple patients, coining the term **vaccination** (from the Latin *vacca*, meaning cow).",
