          "content": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[3.1]</span> In 1797, the Royal Society rejected Jenner’s initial paper due to insufficient evidence. Undeterred, Jenner published his findings privately in 1798 in *An Inquiry into the Causes and Effects of the Variolae Vaccinae*. Despite its brilliance, Jenner encountered ferocious resistance from several fronts.<br><br><span class=\"para-ref\">[3.2]</span> Commercial inoculators opposed vaccination because safe cowpox cost far less than expensive variolation regimes. Religious critics denounced injecting animal matter into humans as sinful, while satirical cartoons depicted vaccinated patients sprouting cow horns and cloven hooves. Crucially, doctors at London Smallpox Hospital carelessly contaminated cowpox samples with live smallpox, causing fatal outbreaks that damaged public confidence. Furthermore, because Jenner could not scientifically explain *how* vaccination worked without Germ Theory, conservative physicians remained deeply skeptical.",
