          "act": 5,
          "title": "Compulsory Vaccination & The Anti-Vaccination League (1852–1871)",
          "theme_heading": "Compulsory Vaccination & The Eradication Legacy (1853–1980)",
          "text": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[5.1]</span> To combat recurring epidemics, Parliament passed the **1853 Compulsory Vaccination Act**, legally requiring parents to vaccinate infants within three months or face hefty fines. While this triggered fierce resistance from the *Anti-Vaccination League*—who protested state intrusion into bodily autonomy—the statistical triumph was undeniable: British smallpox deaths dropped from an average of 40,000 annually in the eighteenth century to virtually zero by the early twentieth century.<br><br><span class=\"para-ref\">[5.2]</span> Jenner’s work represented a monumental turning point: it proved that fatal infectious diseases could be systematically prevented through targeted immunization, establishing the foundation for modern global immunology and culminating in the World Health Organization’s total worldwide eradication of smallpox in 1980.",
