      "hook_text": "In 1850, entering a British hospital was widely regarded as a death sentence. Infirmaries were squalid, airless warehouses where patients lay packed three to a bed on straw mattresses soaked with pus and blood, while surgeons moved between amputations in blood-stiffened frock coats, spreading lethal hospital gangrene. Nurses were untrained, uneducated, and stereotyped as drunken, corrupt domestic drudges. When the Crimean War erupted in 1854, dispatches in The Times shocked Victorian Britain: soldiers were dying in thousands at the military hospital in Scutari, not from Russian artillery, but from lice, typhus, and cholera. Armed with statistical charts, sweeping administrative authority, and an obsession with clean air, Florence Nightingale and volunteer nurse Mary Seacole broke into the male-dominated military establishment, permanently transforming hospital architecture and turning nursing into a respected medical science. How did Florence Nightingale and the sanitary movement transform British hospital conditions and professionalise nursing?",
