          "theme_heading": "Pestilential Wards & Pre-Modern Nursing (c1700–1850)",
          "text": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[1.1]</span> In the first half of the nineteenth century, British hospitals were squalid institutions regarded by the working classes as \"death houses\" of last resort. Wards were cramped, unventilated, and filthy, with patients sharing beds contaminated with bodily fluids (<span class=\"archival-meta-tag\">Source A</span>). Surgeons operated in unwashed frock coats without sterilizing instruments, moving directly from autopsies to living patients. Consequently, post-operative infections known collectively as \"hospitalism\"—especially hospital gangrene and septicaemia—caused catastrophic death rates.<br><br><span class=\"para-ref\">[1.2]</span> Nursing was equally disreputable. Nurses were completely untrained, poorly paid, and widely stereotyped as drunken domestic servants who neglected vulnerable patients. Middle-class women refused the trade. When Britain entered the Crimean War in 1854, eyewitness dispatches in *The Times* ignited public outrage: British soldiers were dying in thousands not from battlefield wounds, but from preventable cholera, dysentery, and gangrene in squalid military hospitals.",
