          "act": 2,
          "title": "Scutari Hospital & The Crimean Sanitary Revolution (1854–1856)",
          "theme_heading": "Scutari Hospital & The Crimean Sanitary Revolution (1854–1856)",
          "text": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[2.1]</span> In October 1854, Secretary at War Sidney Herbert commissioned Florence Nightingale to lead thirty-eight handpicked nurses to the British army hospital at Scutari, Crimea. Nightingale discovered an administrative catastrophe: the hospital was built over a defective sewer, wards were swarming with vermin, and patients lay in their own filth without clean linen or basic medicines.<br><br><span class=\"para-ref\">[2.2]</span> Facing fierce hostility from army doctors who resented civilian women interfering, Nightingale used private donations to purchase three hundred scrubbing brushes, clean bedding, and nutritious food. She organized hospital laundry and forced through deep-cleaning regimes. When a government Sanitary Commission flushed out the sewers in early 1855, mortality plummeted dramatically from **42% to approximately 2%**. Nightingale returned to Britain a celebrated national heroine, though she correctly recognized that sanitary infrastructure—not romantic nursing—had saved soldiers’ lives.",
