              "citation": "Mary Seacole, Wonderful Adventures of Mrs. Seacole in Many Lands, James Blackwood, London, 1857.",
              "content": "I applied to the War Office and to the Nightingale Fund to be sent out as a nurse to the Crimea, but was repeatedly rebuffed... Did these ladies shrink from accepting my aid because by blood and complexion I was of a darker caste? I resolved that if the authorities would not send me, I would go at my own expense. At Spring Hill, two miles from Sevastopol, I built the British Hotel. I had medicines and bandages continually ready. Many a time I attended poor fellows right upon the battlefield under fire, bandaging crushed limbs, staunching bleeding wounds, and administering warm broths and my own herbal cordials to soldiers dying of dysentery and cholera.",
