          "content": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[4.1]</span> In her 1859 book *Notes on Hospitals*, Nightingale revolutionized British hospital architecture. She fiercely condemned large, multi-storey block hospitals as toxic incubators of disease, designing instead the influential **pavilion plan**. Hospitals were arranged into separate, self-contained single-storey wings linked by covered walkways, isolating infectious wards from one another.<br><br><span class=\"para-ref\">[4.2]</span> Each ward featured large windows on opposite walls to ensure constant cross-ventilation, hard impervious floors that could be easily scrubbed, and separate toilet blocks with dedicated drainage. Implemented at the Herbert Hospital in Woolwich and the rebuilt St Thomas’ Hospital in London, the pavilion plan dominated British hospital construction for over half a century, drastically reducing cross-infection and providing a clean, hygienic environment for surgical recovery.",
