      "hook_text": "In 1840, an operating theatre was an arena of pure butchery. With no effective pain relief, patients were pinned down to blood-drenched wooden tables by muscular hospital porters while screaming in terror. The greatest surgeon in London, Robert Liston, could amputate an adult leg in twenty-eight seconds, famously operating in a dark frock coat so encrusted with dried blood that it could stand upright on its own. Yet, when James Young Simpson discovered that sniffing chloroform vapor could plunge a patient into painless unconsciousness, surgery did not become safer—it became far more deadly. During the catastrophic \"Black Period\" of surgery, surgeons operated deeper and longer than ever before, unknowingly carrying lethal gangrene bacteria on their unwashed hands deep into internal organs. It took a quiet Quaker surgeon named Joseph Lister, armed with carbolic acid and Louis Pasteur’s Germ Theory, to conquer the invisible army of wound infection. How did James Simpson's use of chloroform and Joseph Lister's introduction of antiseptics overcome the dual barriers of pain and infection?",
