          "content": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[2.1]</span> In November 1847, **James Young Simpson**, Professor of Midwifery at Edinburgh University, tested various chemicals with his colleagues. Inhaling **chloroform**, they collapsed unconscious under the table, awakening with no ill effects. Simpson immediately championed chloroform for surgery and childbirth.<br><br><span class=\"para-ref\">[2.2]</span> However, chloroform faced fierce resistance. Religious critics argued pain in childbirth was God’s punishment for Eve’s sin, while military surgeons claimed pain kept wounded soldiers alert. Crucially, chloroform was dangerous: without precision dosage, overdoses caused heart failure, illustrated in 1848 when fourteen-year-old Hannah Greener died during minor toenail surgery. Opposition collapsed in 1853 when **Queen Victoria** inhaled chloroform administered by Dr John Snow during the birth of Prince Leopold, granting royal approval and making pain relief fashionable.",
