          "content": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[3.1]</span> Paradoxically, the conquest of pain triggered the disastrous **\"Black Period\" of Surgery (1846–1865)**. Because patients were unconscious and completely still, surgeons attempted far longer, deeper, and more ambitious operations—opening abdominal cavities and amputating deep internal tumours that had previously been unthinkable.<br><br><span class=\"para-ref\">[3.2]</span> However, because surgeons still knew nothing of germs, longer operations dramatically increased exposure to lethal pathogens. Surgeons wore dirty frock coats encrusted with dried pus and blood, used unwashed catgut ligatures, and rarely washed hands between patients. While patients survived the agony of the knife, they died days later from gangrene, pyaemia, and sepsis. Post-operative mortality actually rose, proving that pain relief alone was useless without conquering infection.",
          "theme": "The\"Black Period\" of Surgery & Deep Infections (1846–1865)"
