          "content": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[4.1]</span> In 1865, **Joseph Lister**, Professor of Surgery at Glasgow Royal Infirmary, resolved to conquer surgical sepsis. Reading Louis Pasteur’s 1861 Germ Theory, Lister realized wound sepsis was not caused by oxygen, but by living airborne microbes invading flesh. Hearing that carbolic acid treated smelly municipal sewage in Carlisle, Lister applied it to surgery.<br><br><span class=\"para-ref\">[4.2]</span> In August 1865, Lister treated eleven-year-old Jamie Greenlees, who suffered a compound leg fracture where bone pierced the skin. Instead of amputating, Lister dressed the wound with carbolic acid lint; after six weeks, the bone healed without infection. Lister invented a carbolic spray to sterilize operating theatres, surgical instruments, and dressings (<span class=\"archival-meta-tag\">Source D</span>). Between 1865 and 1869, his surgical mortality dropped dramatically from **46% to 15%**, establishing modern **antiseptic surgery**.",
