          "citation": "James Young Simpson, Account of a New Anaesthetic Agent, as a Substitute for Sulphuric Ether in Surgery and Midwifery, Sutherland and Knox, Edinburgh, 1847.",
          "content": "I have used chloroform in several surgical and obstetric cases with the most complete success. It possesses vast advantages over sulphuric ether: a much smaller quantity is required; its action is far more rapid, pleasant, and complete; it does not irritate the bronchial tubes or produce that distressing cough and headache which follow ether. In an operation performed yesterday by Professor Miller upon a young boy with necrosis of the radius, the patient was rendered entirely insensible in under a minute without the slightest struggle or cry. The diseased bone was cut away, and the wound dressed, while the boy lay in a calm, sweet sleep, waking totally unaware that the knife had touched him.",
