          "title": "Source D: Joseph Lister, 'On the Antiseptic Principle in the Practice of Surgery', 'The Lancet' (21 September 1867)",
          "citation": "Joseph Lister, 'On the Antiseptic Principle in the Practice of Surgery', The Lancet, vol. 90, no. 2299, 1867, pp. 353–356.",
          "content": "In the course of an extended enquiry into the nature of inflammation, the author was struck with the analogy between that condition and the process of fermentation. The research of M. Pasteur had demonstrated that fermentation is caused by living microscopic organisms suspended in the atmosphere... Applying these principles to compound fractures, in which the open flesh admits air, I treated the wounds with carbolic acid, which destroys the vitality of these floating septic particles. Out of eleven consecutive cases of compound fracture treated in Glasgow Royal Infirmary, not a single one developed hospital gangrene, pyæmia, or erysipelas—conditions which previously proved fatal in nearly half our amputations.",
