          "letter": "E",
          "type": "written",
          "title": "Source E: Robert Lawson Tait, Address to the British Medical Association, 'British Medical Journal' (1880)",
          "citation": "Robert Lawson Tait, 'On the Treatment of Pelvic Peritonitis and the Fallacies of the Carbolic Spray', British Medical Journal, 1880.",
          "content": "I have abandoned Professor Lister's carbolic spray entirely, and my success in abdominal surgery has increased rather than diminished. Carbolic acid is a corrosive poison; it damages healthy human tissue, chills the patient, and deadens the surgeon's fingers. The true secret of surgical success is not pouring poisons into wounds to kill germs, but absolute, scrupulous cleanliness. We must scrub the skin with boiling water and soap, boil all our instruments in steam autoclaves, use boiled silk ligatures, and wash our hands until not a speck of filth remains. Aseptic surgery—keeping germs out from the beginning—is far superior to Lister's irritating antiseptic spray.",
