      "hook_text": "In August 1854, the densely packed slums of Soho in London became ground zero for a nightmare. Within ten days, over five hundred healthy people suffered violent vomiting, muscle spasms, and severe diarrhoea, turning a ghostly blue before dying of dehydration in their beds. Orthodox medicine insisted that the killer was miasma—a poisonous, invisible vapor rising from foul rubbish and cesspools. But an anaesthetist named Dr John Snow suspected a far more terrifying reality: Londoners were drinking their own sewage. Going door-to-door through the contaminated streets, Snow marked every corpse as a black bar on a street map, tracing the deaths back to a single iron water pump on Broad Street. Yet, when Snow removed the pump handle and halted the epidemic, the government Board of Health refused to believe him. It took a blazing summer heatwave in 1858—the \"Great Stink\" that made the River Thames smell so vile it brought Parliament to a standstill—to finally force the British state to abandon laissez-faire and build London’s underground sewers. How did John Snow's investigation of cholera and the Great Stink compel the British government to abandon laissez-faire?",
