          "text": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[1.1]</span> In the nineteenth century, Britain was terrorized by four devastating pandemics of **Asiatic cholera** (1831, 1848, 1853, and 1866). Cholera was terrifyingly swift: victims suffered violent watery diarrhea, extreme vomiting, and painful muscle cramps, dying of rapid dehydration within hours. Because medical orthodoxy attributed cholera to atmospheric **miasma**, authorities focused on removing smelly filth from streets.<br><br><span class=\"para-ref\">[1.2]</span> Following Edwin Chadwick’s groundbreaking 1842 report on urban sanitary conditions, Parliament passed the **1848 Public Health Act**, creating a General Board of Health. However, because the Act was purely *permissive* (optional), tight-fisted local town councils refused to spend taxpayers' money constructing clean sewers and water supplies, leaving working-class industrial slums completely unprotected against water-borne disease.",
