          "content": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[3.1]</span> Snow confirmed his water-borne hypothesis on a massive municipal scale through his \"Grand Experiment\" in South London during the 1854 epidemic. Comparing two competing water companies supplying identical streets in Lambeth and Southwark, Snow uncovered stark statistical evidence: customers of the *Southwark and Vauxhall Company* (which drew raw water from the sewage-polluted central Thames) suffered **71 cholera deaths per 10,000 houses**; customers of the *Lambeth Company* (drawing clean water upriver from Thames Ditton) suffered only **5 deaths per 10,000 houses**.<br><br><span class=\"para-ref\">[3.2]</span> Despite Snow’s overwhelming cartographic and statistical proof, the General Board of Health rejected his findings in its 1855 report, stubbornly reaffirming miasma theory. *The Lancet* mocked his \"peculiar water hypothesis.\" Snow died of a stroke in 1858, never living to see his life-saving discovery officially accepted.",
