          "content": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[4.1]</span> Government inaction was finally shattered by the **Great Stink of July 1858**. A scorching summer heatwave caused the River Thames—which carried the raw sewage of two million Londoners—to ferment into an intolerable, putrid stench. Sittings in the House of Commons were suspended, and curtains were soaked in chloride of lime to neutralize the fumes.<br><br><span class=\"para-ref\">[4.2]</span> Terrified for their own survival, Parliament abandoned *laissez-faire* in just sixteen days, funding civil engineer **Sir Joseph Bazalgette** to build London’s underground sewer network (<span class=\"archival-meta-tag\">Source C</span>). Completed in 1865, Bazalgette built 1,100 miles of street sewers connecting to 82 miles of massive brick intercepting tunnels, transporting 420 million gallons of sewage eastward away from drinking intakes. When cholera returned in 1866, areas connected to Bazalgette's sewers remained entirely disease-free, conclusively vindicating Snow’s water-borne theory.",
