          "model_answer": "<p>Source A is highly useful because Edwin Chadwick articulates the foundational argument of the sanitary movement: filth, defective drainage, and contaminated water directly generated epidemic diseases among industrial workers. As an official Poor Law Commission report, it demonstrates why the state began abandoning *laissez-faire* economics to pass the 1848 Public Health Act, even though Chadwick still attributed epidemics to miasma rather than waterborne bacteria.</p>",
          "teacher_notes": {
            "source_context": "In 1842, social reformer Edwin Chadwick published his landmark sanitary survey, linking filth and poverty to epidemic diseases, leading directly to the Public Health Act 1848. **Hinge Question:** How did Edwin Chadwick use empirical evidence to convince parliament that sanitary reform was cheaper than supporting impoverished widows and orphans?"
          },
          "text": "The primary and most important condition that affects the health of the labouring classes is the defective drainage, the lack of clean water, and the accumulation of decaying refuse in their dwellings and streets. Such conditions generate atmospheric impurities that depress the vital powers and produce epidemic, endemic, and contagious diseases. The expenditure of public funds on clean water and underground drainage will prevent disease, reduce pauperism, and promote public morality."
