          "title": "Mendel, Heredity & The Chromosomal Mystery (c1900–1940s)",
          "theme_heading": "Mendel, Heredity & The Chromosomal Mystery (c1900–1940s)",
          "text": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[1.1]</span> By the early twentieth century, Germ Theory explained how external bacteria caused infectious illnesses, but could not explain why hereditary conditions passed between generations. Biologists revived Gregor Mendel’s 1866 pea-plant experiments, establishing that physical traits were inherited through fundamental units named **genes**.<br><br><span class=\"para-ref\">[1.2]</span> Using high-powered electron microscopes in the 1930s, scientists discovered that genes resided inside cell nuclei upon thread-like structures called **chromosomes**. In 1944, Oswald Avery proved that DNA (*deoxyribonucleic acid*) carried genetic information. However, because DNA's molecular structure remained completely unknown, scientists could not explain how genetic code instructed human cells to develop or malfunction.",
