          "theme_heading": "Watson & Crick’s Double Helix Model (1953)",
          "text": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[3.1]</span> At Cambridge University, biologists **James Watson and Francis Crick** worked to construct physical 3D models of DNA. Shown Photograph 51 by Wilkins without Franklin's knowledge, Watson instantly recognized the helical structure.<br><br><span class=\"para-ref\">[3.2]</span> In April 1953, Watson and Crick published their **Double Helix** model in *Nature*. They demonstrated that DNA resembled a twisted ladder: outer phosphate rails linked by four chemical bases—Adenine (A), Thymine (T), Cytosine (C), and Guanine (G)—that paired strictly (A with T, C with G). This pairing revealed how DNA replicates by unzipping, creating the foundation of modern genetics.",
