          "theme_heading": "The Human Genome Project (1990–2003)",
          "text": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[4.1]</span> Deciphering DNA catalyzed the greatest biological endeavor in history: the **Human Genome Project (1990–2003)**. Funded by international governments and the Wellcome Trust, scientists across six nations mapped the entire sequence of human genetic code.<br><br><span class=\"para-ref\">[4.2]</span> Completed in 2003, researchers mapped all **3.2 billion base pairs** composing approximately 20,500 human genes. This provided an open-access \"parts list\" for the human body, allowing doctors to identify faulty genetic mutations responsible for inherited disorders like cystic fibrosis, sickle-cell anaemia, and Huntington’s disease.",
