      "vocab_cloze_text": "For centuries, doctors were baffled by hereditary illnesses that passed down family lines without bacterial infection. In 1952, Rosalind Franklin used [X-ray Crystallography] to create Photograph 51, proving that [DNA (Deoxyribonucleic Acid)] formed a double helix. In 1953, James Watson and Francis Crick built their famous model, discovering the rule of complementary [Base Pairing] (A-T, C-G). Between 1990 and 2003, the [Human Genome Project] sequenced all 3 billion chemical base pairs, allowing modern geneticists to identify specific mutations causing [Hereditary Disease] like cystic fibrosis and breast cancer.",
      "learning_objectives": [
        "Explain how Mendel’s inheritance theory and chromosomes established the search for the chemical gene.",
        "Analyze the contribution of Franklin, Wilkins, Watson, and Crick to the 1953 double helix breakthrough.",
        "Evaluate how the Human Genome Project transformed disease diagnosis and modern personalized medicine."
      ],
