          "text": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[1.1]</span> In 1895, internal diagnosis relied on external physical examination: palpation, pulse-taking, and stethoscopes. In November 1895, German physicist **Wilhelm Röntgen** discovered invisible electromagnetic radiation passing through black cardboard, which he termed **X-Rays**.<br><br><span class=\"para-ref\">[1.2]</span> Passing rays through his wife’s hand onto a photographic plate, Röntgen produced an image revealing bones and her wedding ring (<span class=\"archival-meta-tag\">Source A</span>). Within months, British hospitals adopted X-rays to locate bone fractures and swallowed foreign objects without exploratory surgery. By the First World War, mobile X-ray units located embedded shrapnel on battlefields, establishing diagnostic imaging.",
