          "citation": "Godfrey Hounsfield, 'Computed Medical Imaging', Nobel Lecture in Physiology or Medicine, Stockholm, 1979.",
          "content": "Conventional X-ray techniques suffer from two fundamental limitations. First, it is impossible to separate the superimposed shadow of bones from soft tissue organs lying behind or in front of them; the image is a flattened two-dimensional projection of a three-dimensional body. Second, conventional radiographs are entirely incapable of distinguishing between soft tissues whose chemical densities differ by less than ten per cent... In computed tomography, an X-ray source rotates 360 degrees around the patient, taking hundreds of thousands of readings through narrow planar slices. A digital computer processes these attenuation values, calculating absolute tissue densities and displaying an accurate transverse picture of soft tissues accurate to within one half of one per cent.",
