          "connectives": "Consequently &bull; Furthermore &bull; In particular &bull; However &bull; While diagnostic imaging revolutionized treatment precision, lifestyle education addressed...",
          "model": "A student arguing that diagnostic technology was more important would focus on how X-rays, CT scanners, and MRI machines permanently eliminated blind exploratory surgery. Before Hounsfield’s 1971 CT scanner, doctors had to cut into living skulls and abdomens simply to see what was wrong; scanning allowed pinpoint surgical excision and early tumor diagnosis. Conversely, a student arguing for lifestyle campaigns would demonstrate that scanning only identifies diseases after irreversible damage has taken place. The primary killers of the modern era—coronary heart disease, lung cancer, and diabetes—are caused by behavioral factors like smoking, poor diet, and alcohol. Government intervention, such as the 2007 public smoking ban and anti-obesity guidelines, prevents millions from falling ill in the first place. The most sophisticated historical judgement concludes that while diagnostic machines revolutionized clinical survival for individuals, public lifestyle campaigns exerted a vastly greater statistical impact on national life expectancy by preventing chronic disease at its societal root."
