          "act": 4,
          "title": "Aneurin Bevan & The Launch of the NHS (1945–1948)",
          "theme_heading": "Aneurin Bevan & The Launch of the NHS (1945–1948)",
          "text": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[4.1]</span> Following Labour’s landslide victory in 1945, Minister of Health **Aneurin Bevan** spearheaded the creation of the NHS. Bevan faced furious resistance from the British Medical Association (BMA), representing doctors who feared becoming underpaid civil servants.<br><br><span class=\"para-ref\">[4.2]</span> Bevan pragmatically compromised—famously saying he \"stuffed their mouths with gold\" by permitting senior hospital consultants to retain lucrative private patients. On **5 July 1948**, the **National Health Service (NHS)** launched. For the first time, medical care, hospitalization, prescriptions, and dental treatments were universal and completely free for every British citizen.",
          "image": "/images/bevan_first_day_nhs_1948.jpg",
          "image_alt": "Minister of Health Aneurin Bevan visiting 13-year-old Sylvia Beckingham, the first NHS patient, at Park Hospital, Manchester, on 5 July 1948",
          "caption": "The Launch of the NHS (5 July 1948): Health Minister Aneurin Bevan visits the first NHS patient at Park Hospital, Davyhulme, Manchester, celebrating the principle that medical care should be free at the point of delivery.",
