      "tasks": [
        {
          "type": "two_sided_argument",
          "topic": "The Foundation of the NHS: Universal Healthcare Triumph vs Financial Burden",
          "question": "‘The establishment of the National Health Service in 1948 was the most significant turning point in medical care in the period c1900–present.’ How far do you agree? Explain your answer [16+4 marks].",
          "text": "The 1948 National Health Service: Social Revolution vs Economic Strain",
          "instruction": "GCSE Evaluative Assessment: Weigh the social impact of the NHS in abolishing healthcare inequality against the economic and structural challenges of state-funded medicine.",
          "advancement": {
            "title": "The National Health Service (Social Revolution & Universal Care)",
            "points": [
              "Prior to 1948, millions of working-class women and children were excluded from the 1911 National Insurance Act, suffering untreated illnesses due to poverty.",
              "Aneurin Bevan established healthcare free at the point of delivery financed by central taxation, providing universal access to GPs, hospitals, dentistry, and spectacles.",
              "The NHS centralized hospital administration, raised surgical standards across municipal infirmaries, and eradicated the terror of medical bankruptcy."
            ],
            "starter": "On the one hand, the establishment of the NHS was a momentous turning point because..."
          },
          "limitations": {
            "title": "Limitations & Alternative Breakthroughs (Magic Bullets & Economic Crises)",
            "points": [
              "Healthcare access is useless without effective cures: scientific discoveries like Salvarsan 606 (1909), Prontosil (1932), and Penicillin (1940) provided the medicines that saved lives.",
              "Huge public demand for dentures and spectacles overwhelmed state budgets, forcing the introduction of prescription charges in 1951 and Bevan’s resignation.",
              "Bevan had to compromise with the BMA by \"stuffing their mouths with gold,\" preserving private beds for wealthy consultants inside NHS hospitals."
            ],
            "starter": "On the other hand, the NHS had limitations and relied on scientific breakthroughs because..."
          },
          "connectives": "Consequently &bull; Furthermore &bull; In particular &bull; However &bull; While the NHS democratized access, scientific pharmacology provided the cures...",
          "model": "A student arguing that the NHS was the decisive turning point would focus on the profound transformation of healthcare access. Before 1948, healthcare was a commercial commodity; working-class families who could not afford private doctor fees or voluntary hospital subscriptions delayed treatment, leading to preventable deaths. Bevan’s NHS established that healthcare was a universal civil right free at the point of need. Conversely, a student arguing for alternative factors would demonstrate that state organization cannot cure disease without pharmacological breakthroughs. Paul Ehrlich’s Salvarsan 606 (1909), Domagk’s Prontosil (1932), and Florey and Chain’s penicillin (1940) created the actual cures that made NHS hospitals effective. Furthermore, the 1951 introduction of prescription charges proved that free care faced severe economic limits. The most sophisticated conclusion argues that while scientific magic bullets provided the medical tools, the NHS was the ultimate turning point because it ensured those life-saving tools reached every British citizen regardless of wealth."
        }
      ]
