          "act": 3,
          "title": "Mice Trials & The Tragic Case of Albert Alexander (1940–1941)",
          "theme_heading": "Mice Trials & The Tragic Case of Albert Alexander (1940–1941)",
          "text": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[3.1]</span> In February 1941, the Oxford team tested penicillin on a human patient: police constable **Albert Alexander**, dying from terminal blood poisoning caused by a scratched rose thorn. Penicillin was injected intravenously.<br><br><span class=\"para-ref\">[3.2]</span> Within twenty-four hours, Alexander’s fever broke and his infected wounds healed miraculously. Tragically, the team ran out of purified penicillin despite recycling it from his urine; the infection returned and Alexander died. The trial proved penicillin worked in humans, but required industrial manufacturing.",
