              "question": "Explain how Source C illustrates the impact of the Second World War on the development of penicillin.",
              "model_answer": "Source C illustrates that the urgent crisis of total war turned penicillin from an Oxford laboratory curiosity into an industrial priority. Allied military demand for treating battlefield wound infections spurred US and British governments to fund mass factory fermentation, ensuring hundreds of thousands of soldiers survived catastrophic wounds.",
              "teacher_notes": {
                "source_context": "By D-Day in June 1944, Allied pharmaceutical plants were producing 300 billion units of penicillin a month—enough to treat every single Allied casualty of the Normandy invasion, cutting bacterial wound deaths by 85%. **Hinge Question:** Would penicillin have been industrialized in the 1940s without the wartime financial backing of the American and British governments?"
              },
