          "title": "Peoria & US Industrial Deep-Tank Fermentation (1941–1944)",
          "theme_heading": "Peoria & US Industrial Deep-Tank Fermentation (1941–1944)",
          "text": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[4.1]</span> British pharmaceutical factories, under heavy German bombing during the Blitz, could not mass-produce penicillin. In July 1941, Florey and Heatley travelled to the United States to lobby government and pharmaceutical corporations.<br><br><span class=\"para-ref\">[4.2]</span> At the Department of Agriculture laboratory in Peoria, Illinois, scientists isolated a super-strain of mould on a cantaloupe melon that yielded 200 times more penicillin. US corporations developed **deep-tank fermentation** using corn-steep liquor, manufacturing millions of antibiotic doses by 1944.",
          "image": "/images/florey_chain_apparatus.jpg",
