      "vocab_cloze_text": "In September 1928, Alexander Fleming accidentally discovered that a spore of Penicillium notatum had landed on his staphylococcus plate, creating [Penicillin]. Fleming recognized it as a revolutionary [Antibiotic], but could not chemically isolate it. In 1938, Howard Florey and Ernst Chain assembled a team at Oxford, using [Freeze-Drying] to purify the unstable substance. After proving it cured mice in 1940, they tested it on police constable Albert Alexander in 1941. In July 1941, Florey flew to the United States, where factories used corn steep liquor and [Deep-Tank Fermentation] to manufacture 2.3 million doses in time for the D-Day landings in June 1944. Today, modern doctors face the growing danger of [Antibiotic Resistance].",
