          "text": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[2.1]</span> As hospital thoracic wards filled with dying patients, the Medical Research Council commissioned epidemiologists **Richard Doll and Austin Bradford Hill** to investigate whether tarmac roads, air pollution, or smoking caused the surge.<br><br><span class=\"para-ref\">[2.2]</span> In 1950, comparing hospital cancer patients with healthy controls, Doll and Hill proved smokers were significantly more likely to contract lung cancer. In 1954, their prospective study of 40,000 British doctors established direct statistical causality: heavy smokers were **twenty-four times more likely** to die from lung cancer than non-smokers, disproving industrial air pollution theories.",
