          "theme_heading": "Flanders Terrain & The Ypres Salient (1914–1915)",
          "text": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[1.1]</span> In autumn 1914, the British Expeditionary Force (BEF) established defensive positions in the **Ypres Salient** in Belgian Flanders. A \"salient\" is a vulnerable bulge in the battle line surrounded by the enemy on three sides, exposing British troops to German artillery fire from multiple directions.<br><br><span class=\"para-ref\">[1.2]</span> Geography dictated medical survival. The Germans occupied the surrounding high ground (such as Messines and Passchendaele Ridges), leaving the British in low-lying, waterlogged clay with an exceptionally high water table. Constant rain and relentless artillery bombardment smashed drainage systems, transforming the landscape into deep liquid mud. Stretcher-bearers struggled through waist-deep mire where dropping a wounded soldier meant drowning, severely delaying casualty evacuation to medical posts.",
