          "content": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[2.1]</span> To survive high-explosive artillery and machine-gun fire, both armies dug complex trench networks. Trenches were constructed approximately six to eight feet deep, reinforced with wooden revetments, sandbags, and duckboards laid over muddy sumps.<br><br><span class=\"para-ref\">[2.2]</span> Crucially, trenches were never dug in straight lines. They followed a **zig-zag pattern** divided into short bays by earth barriers called **traverses** (<span class=\"archival-meta-tag\">Source A</span>). If an artillery shell detonated inside a trench, or if enemy attackers breached a section with machine guns, the blast and shrapnel were confined to a single bay rather than sweeping down the entire line. However, this fragmented layout severely hampered stretcher-bearers, who had to navigate awkward right-angle corners while carrying heavy stretchers bearing severely wounded soldiers.",
