          "content": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[4.1]</span> In striking contrast to muddy Flanders, the terrain around **Arras** in northern France consisted of dry, permeable chalk. In late 1916, New Zealand and British tunneling companies connected ancient underground chalk quarries into an immense subterranean fortress, creating eight hundred miles of lighted tunnels.<br><br><span class=\"para-ref\">[4.2]</span> Within this network, the Royal Army Medical Corps (RAMC) constructed **Thompson’s Cave**, a fully equipped underground hospital located right beneath the battlefield. It contained seven hundred hospital beds, operating theatres, mortuaries, electric lighting, and running piped water (<span class=\"archival-meta-tag\">Source B</span>). Situated only 800 yards from the frontline, Thompson's Cave allowed surgeons to operate on horrific trauma cases within minutes of injury, completely sheltered from German artillery during the Battle of Arras in April 1917.",
