          "content": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[1.1]</span> Waterlogged trenches exerted constant hostile pressure on soldiers' health. Standing for days in cold, muddy water in tight boots constricted blood flow. This caused **Trench Foot**, a painful clinical condition where tissues lost circulation, turned numb and blue, and blistered severely.<br><br><span class=\"para-ref\">[1.2]</span> If neglected, trench foot rapidly deteriorated into gangrene, requiring surgical amputation to stop infection entering the bloodstream. During the terrible winter of 1914–1915, over 20,000 British soldiers were evacuated with trench foot. The condition severely threatened military manpower, forcing senior commanders to treat trench hygiene as a matter of strict military discipline.",
          "theme": "The Flanders Water Table & Trench Foot Pathology (1914–1915)"
