          "content": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[2.1]</span> To combat trench foot, the RAMC enforced strict preventive protocols. Every battalion was ordered to establish drying rooms and provide soldiers with three pairs of dry socks per day. Men were ordered to rub their feet with water-repellent **whale oil** daily.<br><br><span class=\"para-ref\">[2.2]</span> Crucially, officers introduced the **\"buddy system\"**: each soldier was made personally responsible for inspecting his partner’s feet twice daily. Trenches were fitted with raised wooden duckboards and drainage sumps. These preventive measures succeeded: by 1917, trench foot admissions fell by over 80%, proving that disciplined hygiene was as critical as surgical intervention in maintaining army strength.",
          "theme": "Preventive Discipline: Whale Oil & The Buddy System (1915–1918)"
