          "content": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[4.1]</span> Sanitation was a constant life-or-death battle. Open latrines, rotting corpses, and horse manure bred swarms of blowflies that contaminated rations, triggering rampant outbreaks of **dysentery** (severe bacterial diarrhea causing rapid dehydration).<br><br><span class=\"para-ref\">[4.2]</span> The RAMC countered by creating specialized *Sanitary Sections*. Strict regulations governed waste: latrines were dug six feet deep, disinfected daily with chloride of lime, and sealed before overflowing. Drinking water was transported in petrol cans and purified with **chloride of lime tablets**. Though soldiers hated the chemical taste, water chlorination successfully prevented catastrophic cholera and typhoid epidemics, ensuring sickness rates remained far lower than in previous wars.",
