          "content": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[1.1]</span> Battlefield wounds on the Western Front were dominated by modern industrial artillery. Unlike clean bullet wounds, **high-explosive artillery shells** detonated into jagged, irregular metal fragments that shredded flesh, shattered bones, and severed major arteries.<br><br><span class=\"para-ref\">[1.2]</span> Artillery fire was responsible for **58% of all battlefield casualties**, while machine-gun and rifle bullets accounted for 39%. Blast concussions also caused severe internal trauma: rupturing lungs and eardrums without leaving external marks. Surviving the blast was only the first challenge: shell fragments tore dirty uniform fabric, mud, and manure deep into open wounds, creating an unprecedented sepsis crisis.",
          "theme": "Artillery Firepower & Jagged Shrapnel Trauma (1914–1918)"
