          "content": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[2.1]</span> Northern France and Flanders had been intensively manured for centuries. Consequently, the soil was saturated with deadly anaerobic bacteria, specifically *Clostridium tetani* (**tetanus**) and *Clostridium perfringens* (**gas gangrene**).<br><br><span class=\"para-ref\">[2.2]</span> Deprived of oxygen inside deep muscle tissue torn by shrapnel, gas gangrene bacteria multiplied with terrifying speed, producing gas bubbles that made flesh crackle beneath the skin and releasing lethal toxins that killed patients within forty-eight hours. Tetanus caused violent muscle spasms and jaw-locking. From late 1914, routine injection of anti-tetanus serum at frontline aid posts conquered tetanus, but gas gangrene remained a deadly menace that required urgent radical surgery.",
          "theme": "Soil Bacteria & The Gas Gangrene Crisis (1914–1918)"
