          "content": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[4.1]</span> On 22 April 1915, during the Second Battle of Ypres, German forces launched the first mass **chemical gas attack**, releasing 5,700 cylinders of greenish **chlorine gas**. Heavier than air, chlorine sank into British trenches, stripping the lining of soldiers' lungs and causing suffocating pulmonary oedema.<br><br><span class=\"para-ref\">[4.2]</span> Soldiers initially improvised by pressing urine-soaked rags over faces, as alkaline urine neutralized acidic chlorine. In late 1915, Germans deployed **phosgene**, an invisible, deadly choking gas six times more potent than chlorine that caused delayed suffocation up to forty-eight hours after exposure. The RAMC established emergency oxygen therapy stations to treat gassed casualties, while British scientists developed protective respirators.",
