          "content": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[1.1]</span> Casualty survival on the Western Front was a race against infection. Frontline retrieval fell to **stretcher-bearers** (four assigned per company). Venturing unarmed into No Man's Land under fire, bearers carried wounded comrades back through muddy zig-zag trenches.<br><br><span class=\"para-ref\">[1.2]</span> Casualties were brought first to the **Regimental Aid Post (RAP)**, located within 200 yards of the frontline in a dug-out or cellar. Staffed by the Regimental Medical Officer, the RAP performed only immediate emergency first aid: applying field dressings, administering anti-tetanus serum, and giving pain relief before sending casualties rearward.",
