          "id": "med_l24_source_e",
          "letter": "E",
          "type": "written",
          "title": "Source E: From an Inspection Report on Advanced Dressing Stations in the Somme Sector (August 1916)",
          "citation": "The National Archives, Kew (WO 95/RAMC Somme Reports / Crown Copyright).",
          "content": "The Advanced Dressing Station at Becourt received 2,400 casualties in the space of forty-eight hours. The station comprises several reinforced sandbag huts and dugouts capable of accommodating sixty lying cases under cover. Three medical officers and twenty-four orderlies worked continuously without pause.\nThe principal work consists of inspecting frontline bandages, adjusting splints, administering warm beef tea and hot cocoa to counter surgical shock, and organizing motor ambulance convoys. Of the 2,400 men admitted, 1,800 were evacuated rearward by motor ambulance within six hours to Casualty Clearing Stations Nos. 34 and 45 at Grovetown, ensuring life-saving surgical intervention before sepsis could take hold.",
          "question_badge": "INTERMEDIATE CARE: ADVANCED DRESSING STATIONS",
