      "specification_anchor": "The work of the RAMC and FANY; the system of transport and stages in the chain of evacuation: RAP, Dressing Stations, Casualty Clearing Stations (CCS), Base Hospitals; ambulances, trains and barges.",
      "vocab_cloze_text": "To handle tens of thousands of battlefield casualties, the Royal Army Medical Corps created the [Chain of Evacuation], systematically moving wounded soldiers from the firestep back to base hospitals. Stretcher-bearers first carried wounded infantry to the [Regimental Aid Post (RAP)], located in a dugout a few hundred yards behind the front line for immediate bandaging and morphine. Critical surgical care was delivered seven to twelve miles to the rear at the [Casualty Clearing Station (CCS)], which was positioned beside railway sidings and equipped with operating rooms and female nurses. Upon arrival, medical officers conducted rapid [Triage], dividing patients into walking wounded, those requiring urgent lifesaving surgery, and the moribund who were beyond surgical help. Driving through artillery barrages, brave volunteer drivers of the [FANY] piloted motor ambulances across shell-cratered roads to keep the supply line of patients flowing smoothly.",
