          "content": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[3.1]</span> In 1914–1915, compound fractures of the **femur** (thigh bone) were among the deadliest injuries on the battlefield, carrying an appalling **80% mortality rate**. As stretcher-bearers carried casualties over rough terrain, broken bone ends ground against each other, severing femoral arteries and triggering fatal blood loss and shock.<br><br><span class=\"para-ref\">[3.2]</span> In late 1915, RAMC surgeon Hugh Owen Thomas’s nephew, **Robert Jones**, introduced the **Thomas Splint** (<span class=\"archival-meta-tag\">Source A</span>). It strapped the leg into rigid mechanical traction, pulling the bone straight and preventing muscle spasms during movement. Deployed forward to aid posts by 1916, the Thomas Splint dramatically dropped compound femur fracture mortality from **80% to just 20%**, marking one of the greatest medical triumphs of the war.",
          "theme": "Mechanical Traction: The Thomas Splint Revolution (Dec 1915–1916)"
