          "content": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[4.1]</span> Locating buried metal shrapnel and bullet fragments was essential before surgeons could perform effective debridement. Probing blindly with metal forceps damaged nerves and spread bacteria deeper into flesh.<br><br><span class=\"para-ref\">[4.2]</span> The RAMC deployed **mobile X-ray units** mounted on motor vans directly to Casualty Clearing Stations (<span class=\"archival-meta-tag\">Source B</span>). Radiographers took two X-ray plates from right angles, calculating the exact three-dimensional coordinates of buried shrapnel. This allowed surgeons to cut directly to the metal fragment with minimal tissue destruction, preventing infection from festering inside deep wounds.",
