          "content": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[2.1]</span> Although sodium citrate prevented clotting, citrated blood deteriorated after a few days. In 1916, American researchers Francis Rous and James Turner discovered that adding a **citrate-glucose solution** allowed red blood cells to survive longer.<br><br><span class=\"para-ref\">[2.2]</span> When stored in glass flasks and packed in ice, blood remained viable for up to **four weeks**. This biological breakthrough meant blood could be collected in advance from healthy soldiers in base camps and transported forward in refrigerated containers before planned offensives, ready for instant transfusion.",
