          "content": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[4.1]</span> Head and brain wounds inflicted by descending shrapnel carried an appalling 50% mortality rate. General anaesthetics caused brain swelling, and rough surgical exploration triggered fatal haemorrhages.<br><br><span class=\"para-ref\">[4.2]</span> American neurosurgeon **Harvey Cushing**, operating at a British CCS during Passchendaele in 1917, revolutionized brain surgery. Cushing operated under **local anaesthetic**, keeping patients conscious to monitor brain function. Using powerful surgical magnets to draw metal shrapnel out of brain tissue and applying suction to remove dead cells, Cushing dramatically reduced brain surgery mortality from **50% to 29%**.",
